::STAT_U06_L04_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Which feature is needed for a binomial situation?

{
=A. Each trial has success or failure#Correct. Binomial situations count successes across repeated success/failure trials.
~B. Every outcome has more than two categories#Binomial trials have two categories.
~C. There is no fixed number of trials#A fixed number of trials is required.
~D. The probability changes every trial#The probability of success should stay the same.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
In the formula expected successes = n × p, what is n?

{
~A. Probability of success#That is p.
=B. Number of trials#Correct. n represents how many repeated trials occur.
~C. Number of failures only#n includes all trials, not only failures.
~D. The final percent#n is a count, not a percent.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
A website has 200 visitors, and each visitor has probability 0.05 of clicking an ad. What is the expected number of clicks?

{
~A. 0.05#This is the probability of one click, not expected clicks.
~B. 5#This would come from 100 visitors, not 200.
=C. 10#Correct. 200 × 0.05 = 10.
~D. 200#This assumes every visitor clicks.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Which context is not binomial?

{
~A. Counting how many of 12 parts are defective#Each part can be defective or not defective.
~B. Counting how many of 20 customers use a coupon#Each customer can use or not use a coupon.
~C. Counting how many of 15 shots are made#Each shot can be made or missed.
=D. Measuring the exact time each runner takes#Correct. Exact time is not a success/failure outcome.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
A machine has probability 0.02 of producing a defective item. In 100 items, what is the expected number defective?

{
=A. 2#Correct. 100 × 0.02 = 2.
~B. 0.02#This is the probability for one item.
~C. 20#This would use 0.20 instead of 0.02.
~D. 100#This assumes every item is defective.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
What does success mean in a binomial situation?

{
~A. Always a good outcome#Success only means the outcome being counted; it may not be good.
=B. The outcome being counted#Correct. Success is defined by the context.
~C. The number of trials#The number of trials is n.
~D. The long-run average#That describes expected value.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
A sales team contacts 40 people. Each has probability 0.25 of responding. What is the expected number of responses?

{
~A. 4#This uses 0.10 instead of 0.25.
~B. 8#This uses 0.20 instead of 0.25.
=C. 10#Correct. 40 × 0.25 = 10.
~D. 40#This assumes every person responds.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Why is "favorite lunch item" usually not a binomial trial?

{
~A. It is about students#Student contexts can be binomial if there are two outcomes.
~B. It has too many numbers#The issue is categories, not numbers.
=C. It has more than two possible categories#Correct. A binomial trial has only success or failure.
~D. It cannot be surveyed#It can be surveyed, but it is not automatically binomial.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
A quiz has 30 questions and the chance of a correct answer is 0.60 for each question. What is n?

{
=A. 30#Correct. There are 30 trials.
~B. 0.60#This is p, not n.
~C. 18#This is the expected number correct.
~D. 60#This confuses 0.60 with 60 trials.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
For 30 questions with probability 0.60 correct, what is the expected number correct?

{
~A. 0.60#This is the probability for one question.
=B. 18#Correct. 30 × 0.60 = 18.
~C. 30#This assumes all answers are correct.
~D. 60#This treats the probability as a count.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
What does failure mean in a binomial situation?

{
~A. The situation did not work mathematically#Failure is a category, not a math error.
~B. The trial should be removed#Failures are part of the binomial count.
=C. The outcome that is not counted as success#Correct. Each trial is either success or failure.
~D. The probability is negative#Probabilities cannot be negative.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Which values identify a binomial expected value calculation?

{
~A. Mean and median#Those are summary statistics, not the binomial expected value inputs.
~B. Slope and intercept#Those belong to linear models.
~C. Radius and diameter#Those are measurement terms, not binomial expected value inputs.
=D. n and p#Correct. Expected successes are found with n × p.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
A player attempts 50 free throws with success probability 0.72. What is the expected number made?

{
=A. 36#Correct. 50 × 0.72 = 36.
~B. 0.72#This is p, not expected successes.
~C. 50#This assumes every shot is made.
~D. 72#This treats 72% as 72 makes.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Which interpretation is strongest for an expected value of 36 made shots?

{
~A. Exactly 36 shots must be made every time#Expected value is not a guarantee.
=B. Over many sets of 50 shots, the average made should be about 36#Correct. This explains long-run average behavior.
~C. The player cannot make more than 36#Expected value is not a maximum.
~D. The probability of one made shot is 36#A probability cannot be 36.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
What is expected successes when n = 80 and p = 0.10?

{
~A. 0.10#This is p, not expected successes.
~B. 4#This would use p = 0.05.
=C. 8#Correct. 80 × 0.10 = 8.
~D. 80#This assumes every trial is a success.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Which situation violates the fixed-trials condition?

{
~A. Inspecting exactly 25 items#This has a fixed number of trials.
~B. Asking exactly 100 people a yes/no question#This has a fixed number of trials.
~C. Taking exactly 12 shots#This has a fixed number of trials.
=D. Continuing calls until someone answers#Correct. The number of trials is not fixed in advance.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
If p = 0.30, what does that mean?

{
=A. The probability of success on one trial is 0.30#Correct. p is the single-trial success probability.
~B. There are 30 trials#That would describe n, not p.
~C. There are exactly 30 successes#p does not guarantee a count.
~D. The expected value is always 0.30#Expected successes also depend on n.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Expected successes for n = 12 and p = 0.25 equals what?

{
~A. 0.25#This is p.
=B. 3#Correct. 12 × 0.25 = 3.
~C. 12#This assumes all trials succeed.
~D. 48#This divides by 0.25 instead of multiplying.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Which statement about binomial expected value is true?

{
~A. It guarantees the exact number of successes#Expected value is not a guarantee.
~B. It ignores the number of trials#The formula uses n.
~C. It only works when p = 0.50#The formula works for any valid p.
=D. It uses both number of trials and probability of success#Correct. Expected successes = n × p.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
A company expects 4% of 500 emails to receive replies. What is the expected number of replies?

{
~A. 4#This treats 4% as 4 replies without using 500.
~B. 5#This uses 1% instead of 4%.
~C. 10#This uses 2% instead of 4%.
=D. 20#Correct. 500 × 0.04 = 20.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Which wording correctly defines success for "counting how many of 25 seeds sprout"?

{
=A. A seed sprouts#Correct. The success is the outcome being counted.
~B. A seed does not sprout#That is the failure if sprouting is being counted.
~C. The number 25#That is n, the number of trials.
~D. The entire garden#That is context, not the success outcome.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
If 25 seeds each have probability 0.80 of sprouting, what is expected successes?

{
~A. 0.80#This is one-trial probability.
=B. 20#Correct. 25 × 0.80 = 20.
~C. 25#This assumes every seed sprouts.
~D. 80#This treats 80% as 80 seeds.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Why is "number of red cards in 5 draws with replacement" binomial if success is red?

{
~A. There are many card suits#The defined outcome is red or not red, so each trial has two categories.
~B. The number of trials changes#The number of draws is fixed at 5.
=C. Each draw can be classified as red or not red, and the probability stays the same with replacement#Correct. Replacement keeps the probability of success constant.
~D. Expected value is impossible#Expected successes can be calculated with n × p.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
What is expected red cards in 5 draws with replacement if p(red) = 0.50?

{
~A. 0.50#This is p, not the expected count.
~B. 1#This is too low for 5 trials at 0.50.
~C. 5#This assumes all draws are red.
=D. 2.5#Correct. 5 × 0.50 = 2.5 expected red cards.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Which final answer best explains a binomial expected value of 2.5?

{
=A. The average number of successes should be about 2.5 over many repeated sets of trials#Correct. This is the long-run interpretation.
~B. Exactly 2.5 successes must occur in one set#A single set cannot have half a success.
~C. There are 2.5 trials total#Expected successes are not the number of trials.
~D. The success probability is 2.5#A probability cannot be greater than 1.
}
